Seam: ChromeContentBrowserClient's navigation throttle registration, after
      ImageNavigationThrottle -- not directly beside HomeBoundaryThrottle's
      own call, whose adjacent hunk this patch's line would otherwise
      collide with (see patches/README.md). Registration order still runs
      the guard after the home boundary: MaybeCreateAndAdd calls add
      throttles to the registry in the order they are called, and this
      function calls HomeBoundaryThrottle's first regardless of where in
      the file PartitionGuardThrottle's own call sits.
Why: content creates a popup without an opener itself, in the default
     partition, and cannot call into arcium/. A throttle is the one seam
     that sees such a navigation before it commits, and it also catches any
     other path that would put a page in the wrong profile's storage --
     silently, since a shared login looks like nothing on screen.
Delegates to: arcium::PartitionGuardThrottle::MaybeCreateAndAdd

diff --git a/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client_navigation_throttles.cc b/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client_navigation_throttles.cc
index 252e0f5814..b3e3f037cf 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client_navigation_throttles.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client_navigation_throttles.cc
@@ -492,6 +492,10 @@
   web_app::WebUIWebAppNavigationThrottle::MaybeCreateAndAdd(registry);
 
   ImageNavigationThrottle::MaybeCreateAndAdd(registry);
+  // Registered after arcium::HomeBoundaryThrottle above: a link click the
+  // home boundary diverts into a new tab never reaches the guard, and the
+  // tab it opens is already in the right storage.
+  arcium::PartitionGuardThrottle::MaybeCreateAndAdd(registry);
 #endif  // !BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID)
 
 #if BUILDFLAG(SAFE_BROWSING_AVAILABLE)
